    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>An appraisal clause in an insurance policy lets you and your insurer settle a disagreement about the <em>amount</em> of a covered loss without going to court. Each side hires an appraiser, the appraisers pick an umpire if needed, and an award signed by any two of the three generally sets the amount. It does not decide whether the loss is covered.</p>
<p>This guide explains how appraisal clause insurance provisions work for Texas homeowners, when to use one, what it costs, which Texas court decisions matter and what changes under new state rules for policies issued or renewed starting March 1, 2027.</p>

<h2>What an Appraisal Clause Is</h2>
<p>Most homeowners policies include an appraisal provision, usually in the conditions section. The Texas Supreme Court has noted that appraisal clauses are included in most forms of property insurance. The typical clause works like this:</p>
<ol>
<li>You and the insurer disagree on the amount of loss.</li>
<li>Either side demands appraisal in writing.</li>
<li>Each side picks a competent, disinterested appraiser and tells the other side who it is, commonly within 20 days.</li>
<li>The two appraisers try to agree on the amount of loss.</li>
<li>If they cannot agree, they submit their differences to an umpire they chose together.</li>
<li>A written award signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss.</li>
</ol>
<p>Appraisal is not a lawsuit, and there is usually no judge, discovery or hearing. The appraisers inspect, compare estimates and negotiate. That is why it is often faster and cheaper than litigation.</p>

<h2>Amount of Loss, Not Coverage</h2>
<p>The most important limit on appraisal is that it decides <strong>how much</strong>, not <strong>whether</strong>. If your insurer denied the claim because it says the damage came from an excluded cause, such as flood or wear and tear, appraisal alone generally will not overturn that.</p>
<p>The line is not always clean, and the leading Texas case shows why. In <em>State Farm Lloyds v. Johnson</em> (2009), a Plano homeowner's roof was hit by hail. The insurer said only the ridge was damaged; the homeowner's roofer said the whole roof needed replacing. The insurer argued that was a causation question outside appraisal. The Texas Supreme Court disagreed and let appraisal go forward, holding that the scope of appraisal is damages, not liability, and that appraisers can consider what damage the covered event caused versus pre-existing conditions. Coverage and liability questions remain for the courts.</p>

<h2>When to Invoke Appraisal vs. Other Options</h2>
<p>Appraisal is one tool among several. Picking the right one depends on what you and the insurer actually disagree about.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Disagreement about price or scope of repairs on a covered loss:</strong> appraisal is often the best fit.</li>
<li><strong>Claim denied as not covered:</strong> appraisal usually cannot fix it; consider a reconsideration request, a TDI complaint or legal advice.</li>
<li><strong>Delays, missed deadlines or unfair handling:</strong> a complaint to the Texas Department of Insurance or claims under the Insurance Code may be more relevant.</li>
<li><strong>Insurer will not respond at all:</strong> document everything and talk to an attorney or licensed public adjuster.</li>
</ul>
<p>Before invoking appraisal, try the simpler steps first: request a re-inspection, send your contractor's estimate and ask the adjuster to explain each difference. If you are not sure how to read the insurer's estimate, our comparison of <a href="/blog/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster/">public adjusters vs. insurance adjusters</a> explains who can help.</p>

<h2>Costs: Who Pays for Appraisal</h2>
<p>Under the appraisal language in many Texas homeowners policies, each party pays its own appraiser and splits the umpire's fee and other appraisal expenses equally. TDI has also noted that parties usually split umpire costs evenly.</p>
<p>Appraiser fees vary. Some charge hourly or a flat fee; others charge a percentage of the award. Get the fee terms in writing, and be cautious with any arrangement that ties the appraiser's pay to the outcome, because many policies require appraisers to be disinterested. For a modest gap between estimates, appraisal costs can eat up much of the difference, so compare the expected cost with the dollars actually in dispute.</p>

<h2>Texas Case Law on Appraisal Awards and Payment</h2>
<p>Three Texas Supreme Court decisions shape what happens around an appraisal:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Case</th><th>Year</th><th>Key takeaway</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><em>State Farm Lloyds v. Johnson</em></td><td>2009</td><td>Appraisal covers the amount of loss, even when it requires deciding what damage the covered event caused; coverage and liability stay with the courts.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em>Ortiz v. State Farm Lloyds</em></td><td>2019</td><td>Paying the appraisal award bars a breach of contract claim for the amount of the covered loss and bad faith claims to the extent the only damages sought are lost policy benefits.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em>Barbara Technologies Corp. v. State Farm Lloyds</em></td><td>2019</td><td>Paying an appraisal award is neither an acknowledgment nor a determination of liability for Prompt Payment Act purposes, so it does not automatically defeat a Chapter 542 claim.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>In practice, this means that once an insurer pays an appraisal award, a homeowner's leftover claims are narrower, but a claim that the insurer paid late under the Prompt Payment of Claims Act may still be possible. How that applies depends on the facts and on later decisions, so get advice from a licensed Texas insurance attorney before relying on it.</p>

<h2>New Texas Appraisal Rules Starting in 2027</h2>
<p>In 2025, Texas passed Senate Bill 458, which added Chapter 1813 to the Insurance Code and requires residential property and personal auto policies to include an appraisal provision. TDI adopted rules to implement it (28 TAC sections 5.9800 to 5.9806), effective November 24, 2026, as listed on TDI's <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/rules/2026/">2026 adopted rules page</a>. The requirements apply to policies delivered, issued or renewed on or after March 1, 2027, and do not apply to Texas Windstorm Insurance Association or commercial policies.</p>
<p>Key points of the new residential rules:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Either side can demand appraisal on its own,</strong> and the policy cannot require the parties to reach an impasse first.</li>
<li><strong>The award is binding on the amount of loss</strong> unless it was made without authority, did not substantially comply with the appraisal provision, or involved fraud, accident or material mistake.</li>
<li><strong>Insurers must send an appraisal process notice</strong> with the claim acceptance or rejection letter, explaining how to demand appraisal and the deadlines.</li>
<li><strong>Timelines:</strong> 20 days for each side to hire an appraiser, 15 days for the appraisers to choose an umpire, 120 days for the appraisers to try to agree, and an award within 240 days of the demand when an umpire is involved.</li>
<li><strong>Umpire appointment:</strong> if appraisers cannot agree on an umpire, either side can ask a court in the county where the property is located to appoint one. If a policy offers appointment by an outside vendor, only the policyholder may choose that option.</li>
<li><strong>Qualifications:</strong> appraisers and umpires must be competent, independent and disinterested, and for dwelling losses must have relevant adjusting, engineering, architecture or construction experience.</li>
<li><strong>Demand deadline:</strong> generally no later than the end of the policy's limitations period for filing suit, with special rules once a pre-suit notice is sent or a lawsuit is filed.</li>
</ul>
<p>The statute describes appraisal as a process solely to determine the amount of loss, so the "amount, not coverage" rule continues.</p>

<h2>How to Invoke Appraisal: Step by Step</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Read your policy's appraisal clause</strong> for the demand method, deadlines and cost terms.</li>
<li><strong>Put your number in writing.</strong> Send the insurer your contractor's or public adjuster's estimate and ask for its final position.</li>
<li><strong>Send a written appraisal demand</strong> by a trackable method. Identify the claim, the disputed amount and the policy provision.</li>
<li><strong>Hire a qualified appraiser</strong> within the policy deadline and give the insurer the appraiser's name and contact information.</li>
<li><strong>Organize your evidence:</strong> photos, measurements, estimates, invoices and the claim diary.</li>
<li><strong>Follow the timeline</strong> for umpire selection and the award, and confirm any agreed changes in writing.</li>
<li><strong>Review the award and payment.</strong> Check how the insurer applies your deductible, prior payments and depreciation.</li>
</ol>
<p>[[product:document-scanner]]</p>
<p>Depreciation is a common source of confusion after an award, especially on roofs. Our <a href="/tools/insurance-claim-depreciation-calculator/">insurance claim depreciation calculator</a> shows how actual cash value and replacement cost differ, and our guide to a <a href="/blog/hail-damage-roof-insurance-claim/">hail damage roof insurance claim</a> covers the roof disputes that most often end up in appraisal.</p>

<h2>Pros and Cons of Appraisal</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Pros</th><th>Cons</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Usually faster and cheaper than a lawsuit</td><td>Does not resolve coverage disputes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decided by people experienced in estimating property damage</td><td>You pay your own appraiser and share the umpire's cost</td></tr>
<tr><td>Either side can start it with a written demand</td><td>The award is generally binding on the amount, even if you think it is low</td></tr>
<tr><td>New Texas rules add deadlines for policies from March 2027</td><td>Paying the award can narrow later contract and bad faith claims</td></tr>
<tr><td>No discovery, depositions or trial</td><td>Limited ability to challenge a mistaken award</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The appraisal clause is the right tool when your insurer agrees the damage is covered but lowballs the price. It is generally faster and cheaper than court, but it decides only the amount, it costs money, and a paid award narrows later claims. Read your policy's clause, document your number, and get professional advice before invoking it on a large claim.</p>
